-- title:
--   Order at the first end is minus the largest dominant index
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, $A\subseteq L$ a valuation subring, and $F$ a field over $L$ that is a curve over $L$ (principal divisors, finite residue extensions, $\Omega_{F/L}$ free of rank one) and essentially of finite type over $L$. Fix two regular prolongations $R_1,R_2$ of $A$ to $F$ with residue fields $\bar F_1,\bar F_2$ over the residue field of $A$ — each a valuation subring of $F$ together with a surjective residue map whose kernel is its maximal ideal, meeting $L$ exactly in $A$, compatible with the residue map of $A$, and such that every nonzero $f\in F$ has an $L$-multiple in the ring with nonzero residue — and places $x_1$ of $\bar F_1$, $x_2$ of $\bar F_2$ over the residue field of $A$ (valuation subrings, proper, principal ideal rings). Assume the following frame. A set $S$ of places of $F/L$, each rational in the sense that $L$ surjects onto its residue field; a local Noetherian subring $\mathcal N_0\subseteq F$ contained in the integers of both $R_1$ and $R_2$; the description of $S$ as the set of places $P$ whose valuation subring contains $\mathcal N_0$ and for which every non-unit $f$ of $\mathcal N_0$ has $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)$ lying in the maximal ideal of $A$; a generation hypothesis saying every $f\in F$ has a nonzero multiplier $b\in\mathcal N_0$ with $fb$ an $L$-linear combination of elements of $\mathcal N_0$; a subring $C\subseteq A$ of constants with $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{L\to F}(C)\subseteq\mathcal N_0$, a nonzero $\varpi\in C$ cutting out the kernel of the residue map of $A$ on $C$ (for $d\in C$, the residue of $d$ vanishes iff $\varpi\mid d$ in $C$), with $C$ a discrete valuation domain over which every element of $A$ is algebraic; linear disjointness, namely that a family $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,n\to L$ linearly independent over $C$ admits no nontrivial relation $\sum_i c_i a_i=0$ with $a_i\in\mathcal N_0$; a rank-one hypothesis that for $a,b\in A$ with $a$ in the maximal ideal and $b\neq0$ some power of $a$ is divisible by $b$; and the hypothesis that every $g\in\mathcal N_0$ becomes a non-unit after subtracting a suitable constant from $C$. Next, a complete discrete valuation domain $W$ with irreducible $\pi$, a ring map $\sigma:W\to\widehat{\mathcal N_0}$ (adic completion at the maximal ideal) sending $\pi$ to the image of $\varpi$, an integer $E\geq1$, and a ring isomorphism $\iota:\widehat{\mathcal N_0}\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\,W\,(\pi^E)$ (the quotient of $W[[U,V]]$ by `uvCrossingIdeal`) carrying $\sigma(o)$ to the constant $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,o$. Finally, the two end laws: if $f\in\mathcal N_0$ has nonzero $R_1$-residue of order $n$ at $x_1$, then $\iota(f)-\gamma\,V^n$ lies in the ideal generated by $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$ and $U$ for some unit $\gamma$, and symmetrically with $R_2,x_2$, $U^m$ and the ideal generated by $\mathrm{const}\,(\pi^E)\,\pi$ and $V$; and node coordinates $x,y\in\mathcal N_0$ with $R_1$-residue of $x$ zero, $\mathrm{ord}_{x_2}$ of the $R_2$-residue of $x$ equal to $1$, $R_2$-residue of $y$ zero, and $\mathrm{ord}_{x_1}$ of the $R_1$-residue of $y$ equal to $1$. Then for every $q\geq1$, every $a\in\mathcal N_0$ and $e\in L$ with $e\cdot a$ in the integers of $R_1$ and nonzero $R_1$-residue, and every pair of power series $(\alpha,\beta)$ over $W$ with $\beta$ of zero constant coefficient and $\mathrm{mk}\,(\pi^E)(\mathrm{inU}\,\alpha+\mathrm{inV}\,\beta)=\iota(a)$, the order at $x_1$ of the $R_1$-residue of $e\cdot a$ equals $-\sup\,\mathrm{dominantIndices}$ of $(\alpha,\beta)$ for the weighted valuation $w\mapsto q\cdot\mathrm{addVal}_W(w)$ and parameters $(qE,qE)$, that is, minus the supremum of the set of integers $n$ at which `termOrder` of $(\alpha,\beta)$ at $n$ equals `repGaussOrder` of $\mathrm{inU}\,\alpha+\mathrm{inV}\,\beta$.
--
--   This is the reader at the first ($V$-branch) end of the node-annulus dictionary: it converts the order of vanishing at the place $x_1$ of a constant-normalised element of $\mathcal N_0$ into minus the largest dominant index of any normal form of its image in the crossing model $W[[U,V]]/(UV-\pi^E)$, thereby covering elements that vanish at the first end and not only units there. It feeds the layered statement on non-negativity of residue orders and the sum formula over the layers of the model.
